    T104
        title       Type gaps closed at every module seam: carriers, handles, snapshots, evidence, casts
        status      done
        priority    high
        description
            Every type Flow hands across a module seam is as narrow as what the producer holds, so a typo or a wrong
            value fails to compile instead of reading undefined, matching nothing or reaching the runtime; every type
            cast is removed or kept as a verified boundary.

            **Why now:** the user found `snapshot.operations` untyped beside a typed state and memory. Four surveys
            (2026-11-19) found one freedom across the API: a producer holds a narrow type and the seam to its consumer
            erases it (a shared erased intermediate such as `Bound`, `Compiled`, `Values` or `Ending`; a string key for
            a typed reference; two copies of one shape). The deep survey maps 21 seams (14 lossy), 9 carrier families
            and 34 new gaps (31 probe-confirmed). Worst: `O.pay.commit(({ memory }) => ({ amnt: memory.cents }))`
            compiles (about 91 sites); `cut.read(foreignRef)` returns undefined and passes (498 sites); `updateMemory`
            may silently rewrite a `Flow.input` field.

            **Read first:** @review.type-gaps-deep (families, seams, cast inventory, probes),
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/definition.ts#Handles,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/contract.ts, code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/snapshot.ts.

            **Approach:** fix the carrier at each seam, once, so one change closes every gap on it (the convergence
            rule); never patch a signature alone. In order:

            1. F9 excess keys: an arrow's returned object is not excess-checked, so `updateMemory` (and other returned
               shapes) accept extra keys; confirm with a Story whether a `Flow.input` field is rewritten (a wrong
               ending, red first), then an exactness check via a `const` parameter.
            2. F1 contract (`Bound` with phantom input, key, success and failure; `transaction`'s key a `const` tuple)
               together with `ContextSlot<V, P>`: typed operation handles (`commit`, `lookup`, `retry`, `cancel`,
