    T113
        title       The typed machine: derived types everywhere user code needs them, no hand-written guesses
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T57, @T131, @T120]
        satisfies   [@plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Machines.O8]
        description
            Every type user code needs from a definition, machine or contract is derived by Flow, so no handler,
            fragment, helper, mapper or snapshot read types an event, memory, ending or key by hand.

            **Why now:** the body type is lost at `machine()` today, so a snapshot cannot type a schedule's `pausedBy`
            or a keyed schedule's send key, and every later Task that adds a snapshot field (props, emit summary,
            fragment summary) would otherwise add its own typing path (breakdown review, the orchestrator, 2026-11-20).
            A survey (2026-11-20) found about 100 hand-typed parameters and 6 copied local types that nothing checks
            against a definition: about 45 ending mappers (`E.X.from((v: {…}) => …)`, `{ why: string }` about 25 times),
            25 memory parameters and 18 event payloads in fragments and helpers outside the `on` literal, and duplicated
            helpers (`byRepo` in `fixtures/sync` and `fixtures/lanes`, `fromAccount` in two transfers machines). One
            class: a type Flow knows, written by hand where inference does not reach; the user widened this Task to
            remove it everywhere (2026-11-20).

            **Read first:** code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/machine.ts, @questions.advisor-questions (question 1),
            @plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Machines.O8, @spec.Turn (R16, R27).

            **Approach:** design the whole Task at once, a type spike first:
            - carry the body type and keep excess-key checks, since inferring the body loses them;
            - a phantom, type-only `Type` on event tokens (`E.X.Type`, the envelope with its payload) and on memory
              tokens (`Def.M.field.Type`), mirroring Schema's `.Type`;
            - an ending mapper's parameter inferred from the operation's contract where `E.X.from` sits inside
              `outcomes(...)`; if the spike shows that needs a new word, the fallback is one exported ending-value type
              per contract, so `{ why: string }` copies become one name. This Task owns the carried type's shape:
              @task.V3.T76 (props), @task.V3.T77 (emit summary),
            @task.V3.T109 (fragment summary) and @task.V3.T106 (rule tokens) only add fields through it.

            May change: `src/`, `tests/types/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `tools/flow-state-lint/`. Evidence: this
            Task's comments.

            False pass: a type that infers on the happy path only → caught by type tests with a mistyped key, a shared
            body such as `PayBody`, and an excess key.
        acceptance
            - A Snapshot is typed by the body plus the definition: `pausedBy` is a literal union of the body's pause
              reasons (Turn R27, @plan.Machines.O8), shown by a type test that replaces the inverted pin
              in `tests/types/schedules.test-d.ts`.
            - A keyed schedule's send mapper receives the typed key (Turn R16), shown by a type test.
            - An unkeyed schedule's send mapper types `key` as `undefined` (two-sided type test).
            - Excess keys in a body still fail to compile, and shared bodies still compile.
            - `E.X.Type` and `Def.M.field.Type` exist as phantom types, each shown by two-sided type tests (the derived
              type equals the payload or field exactly; a wrong field fails to compile).
            - An ending mapper's parameter is the contract's value or error type, shown by two-sided type tests (a wrong
              field fails to compile); or, if the spike rules inference out, each contract exports its ending types and
              the tests assert those.
            - Every site the survey found is refactored to the derived types: the fixtures' and tests' fragments,
              helpers, ending mappers and local `type` copies, with the duplicated helpers moved to one place; `nub run
              check` shows each site still compiles, and a changed payload in a fixture definition breaks every site
              that reads it (one planted type change per kind).
            - A lint rule refuses a hand-written event, memory or ending-value annotation where a derived type exists
              (`{ readonly event: {...} }`, `{ readonly memory: {...} }`, a typed `.from` mapper parameter), with plants
              in its test.
            - `nub run check` and the suite pass.
        comment     2026-11-20T13:01:52Z
            Breakdown review (orchestrator, 2026-11-20, R48): new Task: carries the machine body type into the snapshot.
            Takes T57's two routed type pins (pausedBy as a literal union, Turn R27 and Machines O8; the typed key in a
            keyed schedule's send mapper, Turn R16) from T109's comment of 2026-11-20; T76, T77 and T109 each add their
            field into this one type.
        comment     2026-11-20T13:03:02Z
            Routed from T57's convergence (orchestrator, 2026-11-20): an unkeyed schedule's send mapper still types key
            as ScheduleKey; it should be undefined, two-sided.
        comment     2026-11-20T13:12:05Z
            Breakdown fix (orchestrator, 2026-11-20): owns the carried type's shape and takes the unkeyed send-mapper
            key pin; T76, T77, T109 and T106 only add fields through it.
        comment     2026-11-20T15:10:12Z
            Widened (the user, 2026-11-20): derived types everywhere user code needs them: phantom E.X.Type and
            Def.M.field.Type, ending mappers typed by the contract, every survey site refactored with type tests, and a
            lint guard.
        comment     2026-11-21T18:31:19Z
            [proposed] Now after @task.V3.T131 and @task.V3.T120. First acceptance row: the AnyBody spike
            (@proposal.algo-types H2); until it passes the cast families count as handled at one point. derive.ts gains
