    T118
        title       Schedule issues derived from timer state, proven by sequence tests
        status      in_progress
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@proposal.spec-handover#cohesion-of-all-eleven-designs, @T120]
        satisfies   [@plan.Machines.O5, @plan.Machines.O8]
        description
            Schedule issues are no longer stored: each timer keeps the last outcome of each of its own checks, and the
            snapshot reads issues from those fields, so no later occasion can take or clear another's issue.

            **Why now:** four close reviews of @task.V3.T57 each found an issue or hold lost across occasions (its
            fourth: a hold restored with every pause reason removed stays held forever; an arming issue replaced by
            another check's throw, then cleared while the key stays unarmed). The second failure after convergence hit
            the failure limit; the user accepted Fable's redesign of the issue seam (2026-11-20).

            **Read first:** @proposal.schedule-issues (the design, the R25 text, P1, P2), @task.V3.T57 (its four close
            reviews), code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts, @spec.Turn (R14 to R17, R19, R25, R27).

            **Risk:** the redesign keeps `decide`, the occasions and T57's tables; it must not reopen what they prove.
            The record format moves from 5 to 6, with a step and restore after a definition edit (a reason, key or
            schedule added, removed or renamed).

            **Approach:** the whole Task at once (the Epic's rule). First commit: the exhaustive sequence test (every
            3-step sequence on a two-key fixture, checked after each step against the ledger of owed firings from the
            Spec), red on today's code, with T57's fourth-review B1 and B2 as inverted tests titled by their rules; then
            the requirements review; then the build.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `tools/flow-state-lint/`, @spec.Turn and
            @spec.WriteAhead with `specs.log` notes. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a sequence test whose expected results come from running the code → caught by the ledger derived
            from the Spec before any code, and a blind derivation of a sample of steps. User's read: the new R25 text;
            P1 (a key that leaves or a state that exits drops its issues); P2 (a refused start marked in the record and
            retried on restore, format 6).
        acceptance
            - The sequence test exists, every 3-step sequence on one two-key fixture checked after each step against the
              Spec's ledger of owed firings, and was red on the code before this Task's build.
            - T57's fourth-review B1 (a hold restored with no reasons left) and B2 (an arming issue replaced, then
              cleared while unarmed) pass, each first landed as an inverted test.
            - No schedule issue is stored: a type cannot hold one, a lint rule refuses one, and four planted faults (a
              stored issue, a check writing another's field, a skipped hold step, a lost refused marker) are caught.
            - Turn R25 carries the proposal's text; P1 and P2 are in Turn and WriteAhead R4, each with a
              `specs.log` note.
            - Record format 6 with its step from 5; restore after a reason, key or schedule is added, removed
              or renamed.
            - `nub run check`, the suite, stress and generated Stories over fresh seeds, and `nub run docs:build` pass.
        comment     2026-11-20T16:01:46Z
            State inventory (lead): one state, a schedule timer's check outcomes; today it is split between `Tracked`
            and a stored issue list, which is the freedom (two writers over time). After: each field below is written
            only by its own check.

            - `keys` outcome (per schedule): today an issue at the `keys` slot in `Incarnation.issues`; read
              every occasion.
            - `selection` (`restartOn`): today `Tracked.baseline` plus a stored issue; read by committed turns, a
              context change and a restore (no baseline is saved, WriteAhead R4); a throw keeps the baseline.
            - `reasons`: today `Tracked.pausedBy` plus a stored issue; read every occasion, armed or idle; a throw keeps
              the list.
            - `arming` `Idle | Refused | Armed`: today `Tracked.run` (none or a run); `Refused` has no state, only a
              stored issue. Written by a start (entry, new key, restart, restore's added schedule), `disarmOn`, exit, a
              key leaving, a firing.
            - last firing failure: today only a stored issue (send refused, mapper, step past the limit); written by
              `fired` (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/firings.ts) and the `Fired` occasion.
            - Issue writers today: `reported` via `withIssuesOf` (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts),
              fed by `issuesAfter` (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts); `raised` (`serve.ts`,
              refused turns of other sources); `withIssues` at creation (`incarnations.ts`, a Creation issue on a
              schedule slot).
            - Issue readers today: `pass` feeds stored schedule issues back into `decide` (`schedules.ts`); the snapshot
              (`src/host/snapshot.ts`); context's open-issue check (`context.ts`, Context source only). Not saved in
              the record.
